WEBVTT

a1.1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.783
Patterns in doeff. Every pattern is the same pair: a program that yields effects,

a1.2
00:00:05.783 --> 00:00:09.200
and a handler stack that gives them their meaning.

a2.1
00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.183
Layered architecture.

a2.2
00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:17.283
The domain layer computes, the service layer yields effects for its orders and its cache,

a2.3
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.550
and the API layer logs and tries.

a3.1
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.233
No layer reaches for its dependencies.

a3.2
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:31.400
Their effects travel outward, in this order: Tell, Try, Get Order, Save Order, Cache Put, and Tell.

a4.1
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.483
There is no order eight, so the in-memory handler raises a KeyError.

a4.2
00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:41.050
Try keeps the failure as a value, and the API layer answers with an error.

b1
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:48.600
Effects compose. Retry with backoff combines Try, Tell and Delay in one small loop.

b2.1
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:50.883
The operation fails twice.

b2.2
00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:56.500
The loop waits two seconds, then four, and the third attempt answers at six seconds.

b3.1
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.683
Delay is an effect, so the time handler decides what a second is.

b3.2
00:01:01.683 --> 00:01:05.950
Under sim time handler, the six seconds pass in a few milliseconds.

b4
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:12.500
Under sync time handler, the same program waits six real seconds.

c1
00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:20.300
State lives in a handler, not in the program. Get and Put ask the state handler for its store.

c2
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:29.900
Each run of the state machine moves order seven one step: pending, paid, shipped, completed.

c3
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:37.600
A fourth run finds no step left. Try gives back Err, with the invalid state.

c4.1
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:43.500
Try never rolls the store back: after the failure, the balance stays at zero.

c4.2
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.500
The snapshot pattern puts one hundred back.

d1
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:53.350
Testing. A test is the same program, under a handler stack the test chooses.

d2
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:59.700
The test reads the log and the store from the handlers it installed. Nothing is patched.

d3
00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:07.500
The retry path runs on the virtual clock: six seconds of backoff, and the test passes in a few milliseconds.

e1
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:15.050
Patterns to avoid. A blocking sleep inside a program waits behind the time handler's back.

e2.1
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.367
Even under the virtual clock, time dot sleep holds five real seconds.

e2.2
00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:23.550
Delay of five moves the virtual clock at once.

e3
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.700
The repository's semgrep rule reports the sleep inside the program, as an error.

e4
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:37.900
A side effect done in place is invisible to every handler. Declare it as an effect instead: WriteFile.

e5.1
00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.683
Now a handler gives it its meaning.

e5.2
00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:46.100
The same program writes the file under one handler, and keeps a record under another.

o1
00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:53.250
Patterns in doeff: programs say what they need as effects, and the handler stack decides what each one means.
